 to drop the labels but a great  many people do use them and yet we've  been very successful though with getting  them to i think there is a different  philosophy or belief in the republican  party today at the grassroots level and  on up through the pros  i think you will find the republican  party today is far more  willing  to  see good in uh in other republicans in  the interest of unity and in the  interest of winning  uh there is a great desire we've had our  bloodbath and learned a lesson from it  now the party was virtually out of  existence  just a few years ago  and i don't think that you're going to  uh to have that problem i don't think  people are going to this convention  frozen into an ideological mold  uh as they have been at some time  governor let's not talk about being  frozen um you do say that your political  philosophy comes closer to that of mr  nixon and to mr rockefeller i mean that  is one position you have taken now  now the nixon coordinator in the south  mr callaway  recently said perhaps we can get george  wallace  on our side that's where he belongs  would you feel that  george wallace belongs on the republican  side and would you welcome him in the  republican party  well  i think this is the decision if george  wallace belongs on the republican side  then he should register  but he is still registered as a democrat  and it would seem to me that um  anyone  i've always believed that anyone who  wants to come into the republican party  has come in by virtue of buying our  philosophy  but we don't go out after someone by  virtue of buying theirs  and therefore if he found that he was  compatible and could believe in the  philosophy of the republican party then  i would think that his place was in our  party  under those circumstances under the  circumstances that he subscribed to our  philosophy what do you think he's  compatible with your philosophy  well i think if you look at his record  as a governor you will find a number of  instances where on domestic issues  he has been  right in line with the present  democratic philosophy of of government  control and subsidy and regulation and  so forth which would be contrary  to republican philosophy at the moment  no question that he's expressed some  views particularly in the international  scene regarding americanism and  patriotism and so forth that i'm sure a  few people uh disagree with what about  his position on racist problems well  he seems to have avoided that subject  pretty well i haven't uh heard too many  statements without that i've heard  everything he says or even paid too much  attention to what it but i haven't heard  uh that much has been said that he's  well is what i mean against integration  to make a specific point  well i happen to be one who believes in  first of all i'm i am incapable of  feeling prejudice myself  i do not believe in discrimination  and i  certainly believe in equality of  opportunity for all people  and i believe that the answer to the  problem will come  someday when  all of us in america will stop using  just as we've stopped using the labels  in our party we'll stop hyphenating  ourselves and using a word in front of  american without with a hyphen let's  let's go back to these labels governor  that you object to but most people in  this country  employ in common talk  liberal and conservative  senator kiekel lost to max rafferty and  there doesn't seem much argument  within the republican party that kiko  represented the moderate or liberal wing  and rafferty the conservative perhaps  the right wing what do you make of that  well i recall  during the campaign hearing both of them  comparing  one the contender comparing his views  and the other comparing his vote record  with the voting record of senator george  murphy  and  they came out about even  with the support of the things that  george murphy had stood for  now  i think the the kiko rafferty campaign  there are a lot number of factors first  of 